"""
Project Management Question Bank: exactly 34 Easy, 33 Medium, 33 Hard = 100 questions.
Focus: PMBOK, Agile/Scrum, Critical Path Method, Earned Value Management, Risk, Scope, Quality, and Stakeholder Management.
"""

pm_easy = [
    ("What are the five official Process Groups defined in the PMBOK Guide?", "Initiating, Planning, Executing, Monitoring & Controlling, and Closing", ["Concept, Design, Testing, Deployment, and Maintenance", "Ideation, Budgeting, Staffing, Delivery, and Review", "Scoping, Scheduling, Sourcing, Launching, and Auditing"], "The PMBOK Guide organizes project activities into five universal process groups from project inception to formal closure."),
    ("What is the primary purpose of a Project Charter?", "Formally authorizes the existence of a project and grants the project manager authority", ["Details the day-to-day task assignments of individual developers", "Specifies the final line-item accounting receipts of the project", "Serves as the legally binding vendor procurement contract"], "A Project Charter is issued by the project sponsor to establish the project and authorize resources."),
    ("In project management, what does the acronym 'WBS' stand for?", "Work Breakdown Structure", ["Work Baseline System", "Weighted Budget Schedule", "Weekly Business Summary"], "A Work Breakdown Structure is a hierarchical decomposition of the total scope of work to be carried out."),
    ("What is the smallest, lowest-level deliverable unit in a Work Breakdown Structure (WBS)?", "Work Package", ["Milestone", "Activity block", "Project summary phase"], "The lowest level of the WBS decomposition is called a work package, which can be scheduled, cost-estimated, and monitored."),
    ("What does a Milestone represent in a project schedule?", "A significant point or event in a project with zero duration", ["A task that takes exactly one calendar month to finish", "The final payment made to external sub-contractors", "A high-risk work package that requires double staffing"], "Milestones have zero duration and mark key achievements such as phase completion or deliverable approval."),
    ("What visual horizontal bar chart is universally used to depict project schedules over time?", "Gantt Chart", ["Pareto Chart", "Scatter Diagram", "Ishikawa Diagram"], "Developed by Henry Gantt, Gantt charts visually illustrate project start dates, durations, and end dates against a calendar timeline."),
    ("In the RACI responsibility assignment matrix, what do the letters R-A-C-I stand for?", "Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, and Informed", ["Reported, Approved, Controlled, and Initiated", "Reviewed, Assigned, Checked, and Inspected", "Resource, Action, Constraint, and Impact"], "RACI clarifies team roles: Responsible does work, Accountable owns outcome, Consulted gives input, Informed receives updates."),
    ("In a RACI matrix, how many individuals should ideally be designated as 'Accountable' for any single task?", "Exactly one individual", ["At least two co-leads", "The entire project team", "Zero (it belongs to the steering committee)"], "Accountability cannot be shared; having exactly one accountable person ensures clear ownership and eliminates finger-pointing."),
    ("What is 'Scope Creep' in project management?", "The uncontrolled gradual expansion of project scope without adjustments to time, cost, and resources", ["The scheduled delivery of features ahead of deadline", "The systematic reduction of features to cut project budgets", "The migration of code between development servers"], "Scope creep occurs when new requirements are added informally without formal change control or budget/schedule revisions."),
    ("In Earned Value Management (EVM), what does 'PV' represent?", "Planned Value (authorized budget assigned to scheduled work)", ["Past Value", "Procured Volume", "Profit Variance"], "Planned Value (PV) is the approved budget assigned to work scheduled to be completed by a specific date."),
    ("In Earned Value Management (EVM), what does 'EV' represent?", "Earned Value (the measure of work performed expressed in terms of budget authorized)", ["Estimated Variance", "Expected Volume", "Enterprise Value"], "Earned Value (EV) measures the actual value of work accomplished against the original planned budget."),
    ("In Earned Value Management (EVM), what does 'AC' stand for?", "Actual Cost (the realized cost incurred for the work performed)", ["Annual Capital", "Allocated Credit", "Audited Charges"], "Actual Cost (AC) is the total cost incurred in completing work during a given time period."),
    ("What is the formula to calculate Cost Variance (CV) in Earned Value Management?", "CV = EV - AC", ["CV = PV - AC", "CV = AC - EV", "CV = EV / AC"], "Cost Variance is the difference between Earned Value and Actual Cost; a positive CV indicates under-budget performance."),
    ("What is the formula to calculate Schedule Variance (SV) in Earned Value Management?", "SV = EV - PV", ["SV = PV - EV", "SV = EV / PV", "SV = AC - PV"], "Schedule Variance is Earned Value minus Planned Value; positive SV means the project is ahead of schedule."),
    ("What does a Cost Performance Index (CPI) greater than 1.0 (CPI > 1.0) indicate?", "The project is currently performing under budget", ["The project is spending more money than budgeted", "The project is running behind schedule", "The project has run out of operating capital"], "A CPI greater than 1.0 indicates that the project is earning more value than the actual costs incurred (under budget)."),
    ("What does a Schedule Performance Index (SPI) greater than 1.0 (SPI > 1.0) indicate?", "The project is progressing faster than scheduled (ahead of schedule)", ["The project is falling behind schedule", "The project is exceeding its budget limits", "The project schedule has been cancelled"], "An SPI above 1.0 means project work is proceeding at a faster rate than originally planned."),
    ("What is the 'Critical Path' in a project network diagram?", "The longest sequence of dependent activities that determines the shortest possible project duration", ["The sequence of activities with the highest financial budget", "The set of non-essential activities that can be postponed indefinitely", "The path containing only external third-party contractor tasks"], "The Critical Path has zero total float; any delay in critical path activities directly delays the project completion date."),
    ("What is 'Float' or 'Slack' time in a project schedule?", "The amount of time an activity can be delayed without delaying the project completion date", ["The extra vacation days accumulated by team members", "The time lost due to unexpected hardware system crashes", "The time buffer between morning and afternoon meetings"], "Float is the flexibility available to delay a task without impacting subsequent milestones or the project finish date."),
    ("What is the total float of activities that lie on the Critical Path?", "Zero (0)", ["Positive 10 days", "Negative 5 days", "Equal to the total project duration"], "Critical path activities by definition have zero total float because any delay directly postpones project delivery."),
    ("Which schedule compression technique adds extra resources to critical path activities to shorten duration?", "Crashing", ["Fast-tracking", "Resource leveling", "Scope trimming"], "Crashing injects additional labor, overtime, or equipment to critical tasks, typically increasing overall project cost."),
    ("Which schedule compression technique performs sequential critical path activities in parallel?", "Fast-tracking", ["Crashing", "Resource smoothing", "De-scoping"], "Fast-tracking overlaps phases or tasks that were originally planned in sequence, which increases project rework risk."),
    ("In Agile and Scrum methodologies, what is a 'Sprint'?", "A time-boxed iteration (usually 1 to 4 weeks) during which a working product increment is created", ["A daily 5-minute meeting where developers report blockers", "The emergency rush phase occurring right before project launch", "A marathon coding hackathon conducted over a weekend"], "A sprint is a fixed-length iteration in Scrum where the team delivers a potentially releasable product increment."),
    ("In Scrum, who is primarily responsible for maximizing the business value of the product and managing the Product Backlog?", "Product Owner", ["Scrum Master", "Lead Software Architect", "Project Sponsor"], "The Product Owner prioritizes user stories in the product backlog to ensure the highest business value is delivered first."),
    ("In Scrum, what is the primary role of the Scrum Master?", "Serving as a servant-leader and coach who removes impediments for the team", ["Assigning daily tasks to individual developers", "Setting the retail selling price of the software product", "Conducting annual performance reviews of developers"], "The Scrum Master facilitates ceremonies, shields the team from external distractions, and resolves blockers."),
    ("What is the recommended maximum duration of the Daily Scrum (Daily Standup) meeting?", "15 minutes", ["45 minutes", "1 hour", "5 minutes"], "Daily Scrum is strictly time-boxed to 15 minutes to synchronize activities and identify blockers without lengthy discussions."),
    ("What are the three standard questions answered by team members during a Daily Scrum standup?", "What I did yesterday, what I will do today, and what impediments are in my way", ["How much budget was spent, who made mistakes, and when will we launch", "What the client emailed, how many lines of code were written, and coffee choices", "What features are cancelled, who needs a vacation, and contract terms"], "These three questions maintain transparency, align daily focus, and highlight impediments requiring Scrum Master help."),
    ("What is an Ishikawa diagram commonly known as in root cause analysis?", "Fishbone Diagram (or Cause-and-Effect Diagram)", ["Spider Web Diagram", "Control Chart", "Scatter Matrix"], "Created by Kaoru Ishikawa, the fishbone diagram visualizes factors contributing to an defect or effect."),
    ("What does the Pareto Principle (80/20 Rule) state in quality management?", "Roughly 80% of project problems or defects originate from 20% of causes", ["80% of project budget must be spent on the first 20% of tasks", "80% of team members produce only 20% of the total output", "Projects always run 80 days late and 20% over budget"], "Pareto analysis helps project teams prioritize efforts by focusing on the vital few causes generating most defects."),
    ("What tool is used to identify, analyze, monitor, and document risks throughout a project lifecycle?", "Risk Register", ["Purchase Order", "Statement of Work", "Burndown Chart"], "A Risk Register logs identified risks, potential impacts, probability ratings, risk owners, and planned responses."),
    ("In Agile, what visual chart displays the amount of remaining work versus time in a sprint?", "Burndown Chart", ["Gantt Chart", "Run Chart", "Kanban board"], "A Sprint Burndown Chart plots remaining story points day by day, showing whether the team is on track to complete the sprint backlog."),
    ("What Agile visual tool uses columns such as 'To Do', 'In Progress', and 'Done' to manage work-in-progress?", "Kanban Board", ["WBS Tree", "PERT Network", "Fishbone Chart"], "Kanban boards visualize workflows and apply Work In Progress (WIP) limits to eliminate bottlenecks and optimize flow."),
    ("In project procurement, what does 'RFP' stand for?", "Request for Proposal", ["Required Financial Payment", "Realized Forecast Plan", "Resource Funding Protocol"], "An RFP is a formal procurement document inviting prospective vendors to submit proposals outlining how they will solve a project need."),
    ("What is a 'Statement of Work' (SOW) in project procurement?", "A narrative description of products, services, or results to be delivered by a vendor under contract", ["A weekly timesheet filled out by project employees", "The financial balance sheet of the contractor", "The formal certificate of project completion"], "The SOW outlines specifications, deliverables, standards, and acceptance criteria required from an external supplier."),
    ("What is the primary objective of a 'Lessons Learned' session conducted during project closing?", "To document successes, failures, and recommendations to improve performance on future projects", ["To assign blame and penalize underperforming team members", "To recalculate corporate income taxes for the past fiscal quarter", "To audit personal expense reports of project managers"], "Lessons learned capture organizational process assets, preserving valuable experiential knowledge for future initiatives.")
]

pm_med = [
    ("In PERT (Program Evaluation and Review Technique), what is the formula to calculate the Beta PERT Expected Duration (Te)?", "Te = (Optimistic + 4 * Most Likely + Pessimistic) / 6", ["Te = (Optimistic + Most Likely + Pessimistic) / 3", "Te = (Optimistic + 2 * Most Likely + Pessimistic) / 4", "Te = (Optimistic + 6 * Most Likely + Pessimistic) / 8"], "The Beta PERT distribution weights the most likely estimate by 4 to calculate a realistic weighted average duration."),
    ("What is the formula to calculate the standard deviation (sigma) of an activity in PERT estimation?", "Sigma = (Pessimistic - Optimistic) / 6", ["Sigma = (Pessimistic + Optimistic) / 2", "Sigma = (Pessimistic - Most Likely) / 3", "Sigma = (Most Likely - Optimistic) / 4"], "The standard deviation measures estimation uncertainty, representing the spread across six standard deviations."),
    ("If a project has an Earned Value (EV) of $80,000 and an Actual Cost (AC) of $100,000, what is the Cost Performance Index (CPI)?", "0.80", ["1.25", "0.20", "-$20,000"], "CPI = EV / AC = 80,000 / 100,000 = 0.80, indicating the project is earning 80 cents of value for every dollar spent."),
    ("If a project has an Earned Value (EV) of $90,000 and a Planned Value (PV) of $100,000, what is the Schedule Performance Index (SPI)?", "0.90", ["1.11", "-$10,000", "0.10"], "SPI = EV / PV = 90,000 / 100,000 = 0.90, indicating the project is progressing at 90% of the planned schedule rate."),
    ("In Earned Value Management, what is the formula to calculate Estimate at Completion (EAC) assuming future cost performance remains at current CPI?", "EAC = BAC / CPI", ["EAC = BAC - EV", "EAC = AC + (BAC - EV)", "EAC = PV * CPI"], "When current cost variances are expected to persist, EAC is calculated by dividing the Budget at Completion (BAC) by CPI."),
    ("What is the difference between 'Free Float' and 'Total Float'?", "Free Float delays an activity without delaying the immediate successor; Total Float without delaying project completion", ["Free Float applies to critical path; Total Float applies to non-critical path", "Free Float involves cost savings; Total Float involves time extensions", "Free Float is calculated by the client; Total Float is calculated by the team"], "Total Float measures flexibility regarding the project finish date, whereas Free Float measures flexibility regarding successor start dates."),
    ("What is the primary distinction between 'Resource Leveling' and 'Resource Smoothing'?", "Resource Leveling can alter the critical path and project finish date; Resource Smoothing never alters the critical path", ["Resource Leveling reduces costs; Resource Smoothing increases staffing", "Resource Leveling is only used in Agile; Resource Smoothing in Waterfall", "Resource Leveling applies to equipment; Resource Smoothing applies to people"], "Resource leveling resolves over-allocations by shifting dates (often extending duration), while smoothing operates within existing float."),
    ("In project risk management, what is 'Expected Monetary Value' (EMV) calculated as?", "EMV = Probability of Risk Event * Monetary Impact of Risk Event", ["EMV = Total Project Budget / Number of Identified Risks", "EMV = Actual Cost - Planned Value", "EMV = Highest Possible Loss + Lowest Possible Gain"], "EMV is a statistical technique multiplying probability by dollar impact to quantify financial risk exposure."),
    ("Which risk response strategy involves paying an external third party to take on the financial consequences of a threat?", "Risk Transference (e.g., purchasing insurance or performance bonds)", ["Risk Mitigation", "Risk Avoidance", "Risk Acceptance"], "Transference shifts liability or financial impact to a third party through insurance, warranties, guarantees, or contracts."),
    ("What risk response strategy is being used when a project team redesigns a system to completely eliminate a hazardous chemical?", "Risk Avoidance", ["Risk Acceptance", "Risk Transference", "Risk Mitigation"], "Risk avoidance changes the project management plan to completely eradicate the condition that gives rise to the risk."),
    ("What is the difference between a 'Contingency Reserve' and a 'Management Reserve'?", "Contingency Reserve is for 'known unknowns' managed by PM; Management Reserve is for 'unknown unknowns' managed by sponsor", ["Contingency Reserve is money; Management Reserve is extra calendar days", "Contingency Reserve is for vendor bonuses; Management Reserve is for payroll", "Contingency Reserve is part of scope; Management Reserve is outside scope"], "Contingency reserves address identified risks in the risk register; management reserves address unforeseen events requiring scope change."),
    ("In Scrum, what ceremony is held at the end of a sprint for the team to inspect their process and identify continuous improvements?", "Sprint Retrospective", ["Sprint Review (Demo)", "Sprint Planning", "Daily Scrum"], "Sprint Retrospective is dedicated to internal team process evaluation (what went well, what can be improved, action items)."),
    ("What is the primary difference between a 'Sprint Review' and a 'Sprint Retrospective' in Scrum?", "Sprint Review demonstrates the product increment to stakeholders; Sprint Retrospective focuses on internal team process improvement", ["Sprint Review discusses team salaries; Sprint Retrospective discusses client invoices", "Sprint Review plans the next sprint backlog; Sprint Retrospective writes test scripts", "Sprint Review occurs daily; Sprint Retrospective occurs once a year"], "The Review inspects the deliverable with stakeholders; the Retrospective inspects team teamwork, tools, and process internally."),
    ("In Scrum, what does the 'Definition of Done' (DoD) signify?", "An agreed checklist of technical criteria that must be satisfied before a user story can be considered complete", ["The final date when the software contract expires", "The moment when the product manager accepts the initial budget", "The point when the team has written 100% of code comments"], "DoD ensures quality standards (e.g., code reviewed, unit tested, documentation updated, integrated into main branch)."),
    ("What does 'Velocity' measure in Agile project management?", "The average number of story points completed and accepted by the team per sprint", ["The typing speed of software engineers in lines of code per hour", "The turnaround time of customer support tickets", "The speed at which the project manager approves expense claims"], "Velocity tracks historical team delivery capacity across sprints, aiding realistic future sprint and release forecasting."),
    ("What estimation technique uses Fibonacci sequence cards (1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13) to collaboratively estimate relative effort?", "Planning Poker", ["Wideband Delphi", "Parametric Estimating", "Analogous Estimating"], "Planning Poker leverages gamified consensus to estimate user story complexity while preventing individual anchoring bias."),
    ("In project quality management, what is the primary distinction between 'Quality Assurance' (QA) and 'Quality Control' (QC)?", "QA is process-oriented (preventing defects); QC is product-oriented (detecting defects in finished deliverables)", ["QA is performed by developers; QC is performed by the marketing team", "QA tests software hardware; QC audits financial accounting balances", "QA occurs after release; QC occurs before development starts"], "QA establishes robust processes and audits to build quality in; QC inspects deliverables to catch defects before delivery."),
    ("In Statistical Process Control, what do Upper and Lower Control Limits (UCL and LCL) represent on a Control Chart?", "Statistically determined thresholds (typically +/- 3 sigma) representing the boundaries of normal process variation", ["The maximum and minimum budget allowable for a task", "The client-specified design tolerance limits in the contract", "The daily working hours of testing personnel"], "Control limits indicate whether a process is stable and in statistical control, based on natural process capability."),
    ("In Tuckman's model of team development, what is the 'Storming' phase characterized by?", "Conflict, competition, and friction as team members confront differing ideas and challenge authority", ["Polite introductions and exploratory social conversations", "High performance where tasks are accomplished seamlessly", "Establishment of mutual trust and collaborative team norms"], "Storming is the turbulent second stage where personal boundaries and leadership dynamics are contested before norms form."),
    ("In Tuckman's model of team development, what occurs during the 'Norming' stage?", "Team members resolve differences, establish shared working agreements, and develop mutual trust", ["The team disbands following project completion", "Severe interpersonal conflicts cause team dissolution", "The team starts from scratch with new requirements"], "During Norming, roles settle, collaboration deepens, and team cohesion stabilizes around shared operating rules."),
    ("Which type of contract places the highest financial risk on the seller (contractor)?", "Firm Fixed Price (FFP)", ["Cost Plus Fixed Fee (CPFF)", "Time and Materials (T&M)", "Cost Plus Percentage of Cost (CPPC)"], "In FFP contracts, the seller is legally obligated to deliver the scope for an agreed sum; any cost overrun reduces seller profit."),
    ("Which contract type places the highest cost risk on the buyer (customer)?", "Cost Reimbursable (such as Cost Plus Percentage of Cost)", ["Firm Fixed Price (FFP)", "Fixed Price with Economic Price Adjustment", "Fixed Price Incentive Fee"], "Cost reimbursable contracts obligate the buyer to pay all incurred vendor costs, creating minimal incentive for vendor cost control."),
    ("What is 'Parametric Estimating' in project management?", "An estimation technique that uses statistical relationships between historical data and variables (e.g., cost per square foot)", ["Estimating by asking three executive stakeholders for intuition", "Comparing the current project to one previous identical project", "Breaking tasks down to the minute work package level"], "Parametric estimating calculates cost or duration by multiplying project metrics (e.g., number of cables, square meters) by unit rates."),
    ("What is 'Analogous Estimating' (Top-Down Estimating)?", "Estimating cost or duration using historical values from previous similar projects as a basis", ["Using mathematical algorithms and computer simulations", "Conducting a formal tender among external contractors", "Adding together individual bottom-up work packages"], "Analogous estimation is quick and inexpensive, utilized in early stages when detailed project information is limited."),
    ("In project schedule network diagrams, what is a 'Lead'?", "The amount of time a successor activity can be advanced with respect to a predecessor activity", ["The delay required between two sequential activities", "The senior engineer responsible for code deployment", "The slack time remaining before the project finish date"], "A lead is an acceleration of the successor task (e.g., starting wallpapering 2 days before painting finishes)."),
    ("In project schedule network diagrams, what is a 'Lag'?", "A directed delay inserted between a predecessor and successor activity (e.g., waiting for concrete to cure)", ["An accidental scheduling mistake made by project planners", "The negative variance on an Earned Value chart", "The acceleration of a task ahead of its planned start"], "A lag forces a waiting period between dependent tasks, such as allowing 3 days for paint drying before plastering."),
    ("What is the primary role of a 'Change Control Board' (CCB)?", "A formally constituted group responsible for reviewing, evaluating, approving, or rejecting proposed project changes", ["A labor union committee handling employee workplace grievances", "The corporate board of directors auditing annual dividends", "The IT department that resets employee network passwords"], "The CCB ensures that no change to baseline scope, cost, or schedule occurs without rigorous evaluation and stakeholder impact review."),
    ("In stakeholder management, what does a 'Power/Interest Grid' help project managers determine?", "The appropriate communication and engagement strategy for different stakeholder groups", ["The exact bonus pay distribution among executives", "The technical architecture of the underlying cloud databases", "The sequence of critical path tasks in project networks"], "Stakeholders with high power and high interest are managed closely, whereas low power and low interest receive minimal monitoring."),
    ("What does a To-Complete Performance Index (TCPI) of 1.25 signify?", "The remaining project work must achieve a cost efficiency of 125% to finish within the approved budget", ["The project is 25% ahead of the original baseline schedule", "The team will finish the project with a 25% surplus budget", "The project manager should immediately cancel 25% of scope"], "TCPI > 1.0 means future work must be performed with higher efficiency than originally planned to meet the target budget."),
    ("In configuration management, what is a 'Baseline'?", "The approved version of a work product (scope, cost, schedule) that can only be changed through formal change control", ["The worst-case financial performance scenario", "The minimum qualification required for hiring project staff", "The lowest grade of materials allowed by safety regulations"], "Baselines (Scope Baseline, Cost Baseline, Schedule Baseline) serve as the standard comparison points for performance measurement."),
    ("What is 'Gold Plating' in project scope management?", "Giving the customer extra features or higher quality than agreed upon without formal approval", ["Applying real gold leafing to product exterior packaging", "Delivering a luxury hardware version to corporate sponsors", "Achieving zero defects across all software modules"], "Gold plating introduces unnecessary risk, unauthorized costs, and potential defects; professional PM principles advise against it."),
    ("What is a 'Secondary Risk' in project risk management?", "A new risk that arises as a direct consequence of implementing a risk response", ["A risk of negligible financial consequence", "A risk that only affects internal junior team members", "A risk that occurs after the project warranty period ends"], "Secondary risks must be analyzed during risk response planning (e.g., installing a backup generator introduces fuel storage fire risk)."),
    ("What is a 'Residual Risk' in risk management?", "The risk that remains after risk response strategies have been implemented", ["A risk that has completely disappeared from the project", "The risk incurred by sub-contractors after project handover", "A risk that occurred in the previous fiscal year"], "Residual risks are acknowledged and accepted; project teams maintain contingency reserves to absorb residual risk impacts.")
]

pm_hard = [
    ("In Earned Value Management, what is the formula to calculate Variance at Completion (VAC)?", "VAC = BAC - EAC", ["VAC = EAC - BAC", "VAC = AC - EV", "VAC = PV - AC"], "Variance at Completion is the difference between Budget at Completion and Estimate at Completion; positive VAC means favorable."),
    ("If BAC = $200,000, EV = $100,000, AC = $120,000, and PV = $110,000, what is the TCPI to achieve the original BAC?", "1.25", ["0.83", "1.00", "1.10"], "TCPI_BAC = (BAC - EV) / (BAC - AC) = (200,000 - 100,000) / (200,000 - 120,000) = 100,000 / 80,000 = 1.25."),
    ("When calculating EAC under the assumption that both CPI and SPI influence future work, what is the correct formula?", "EAC = AC + [(BAC - EV) / (CPI * SPI)]", ["EAC = BAC / (CPI + SPI)", "EAC = AC + (BAC - PV)", "EAC = (BAC - EV) / (CPI - SPI)"], "This composite formula adjusts the remaining work by both cost efficiency (CPI) and schedule efficiency (SPI), reflecting combined pressure."),
    ("In project schedule analysis, what condition results in a 'Negative Float' on a network diagram?", "When a fixed contractual completion deadline is earlier than the calculated early finish date of the critical path", ["When project activities finish earlier than planned", "When resources work without taking scheduled lunch breaks", "When total budget exceeds earned value by more than 10%"], "Negative float indicates that the project is mathematically in violation of an imposed constraint date, requiring urgent schedule compression."),
    ("In Earned Schedule (ES) theory, how is Earned Schedule calculated from the Earned Value and Planned Value curves?", "By determining the point in time where the current Earned Value equals the Planned Value curve", ["By multiplying the actual calendar days by the CPI index", "By dividing the critical path duration by total team velocity", "By taking the derivative of cumulative Actual Cost"], "Earned Schedule extends EVM to time units, preventing the mathematical flaw where SPI converges to 1.0 at project completion regardless of lateness."),
    ("What is the primary formula for Schedule Performance Index based on time (SPI_t) in Earned Schedule management?", "SPI_t = ES / AT (where ES is Earned Schedule and AT is Actual Time)", ["SPI_t = AT / ES", "SPI_t = (ES - AT) / BAC", "SPI_t = EV / AC"], "SPI_t accurately measures time-based schedule efficiency without collapsing back to 1.0 when a project finishes late."),
    ("In project risk analysis, what does a Monte Carlo simulation generate?", "A cumulative probability distribution of project completion dates and total costs through thousands of iterations", ["A single deterministic schedule date with 100% guarantee", "The exact list of contractor personnel who will commit errors", "A deterministic Gantt chart with fixed float values"], "Monte Carlo randomly samples probability distributions for each task to calculate the likelihood of meeting specific cost/time targets."),
    ("In a project network diagram, what is 'Path Convergence' and why does it represent significant risk?", "Multiple predecessor activities merge into a single successor; the successor is delayed if ANY predecessor is delayed", ["Two critical paths run parallel without ever meeting", "A single task splits into multiple independent threads", "Activities share the same geographic physical work site"], "Path convergence magnifies risk because the probability of on-time start for the successor is the product of all predecessor probabilities."),
    ("In Critical Chain Project Management (CCPM), what is the 'Project Buffer'?", "A consolidated contingency buffer placed at the very end of the critical chain to protect the final delivery date", ["Extra money added to individual department line items", "A waiting room built for project stakeholders during reviews", "A five-day holiday granted to developers after every sprint"], "CCPM strips safety margins from individual tasks and aggregates them into strategic project and feeding buffers."),
    ("In Critical Chain Project Management (CCPM), where are 'Feeding Buffers' positioned?", "At points where non-critical chains merge into the critical chain", ["Directly after the kickoff meeting", "At the very end of the project lifecycle", "Inside the executive management contingency reserve"], "Feeding buffers protect the critical chain from delays occurring on feeder (non-critical) paths."),
    ("In quality management, what is the defect rate corresponding to a 'Six Sigma' process capability (accounting for 1.5 sigma drift)?", "3.4 defects per million opportunities (DPMO)", ["6.0 defects per thousand parts", "66,807 defects per million opportunities", "0.0001 defects per billion opportunities"], "A Six Sigma quality process yields 99.99966% defect-free output, translating mathematically to 3.4 DPMO with standard 1.5 sigma shift."),
    ("What does the 'Taguchi Loss Function' postulate regarding quality and customer satisfaction?", "Financial loss to society begins whenever a product parameter deviates from target, even within tolerance limits", ["Loss only occurs when a product is completely rejected at inspection", "Quality improves linearly with the amount of money spent on marketing", "Zero defects guarantees 100% profitability in competitive markets"], "Genichi Taguchi showed that customer dissatisfaction increases quadratically as characteristics drift from the nominal target value."),
    ("In project procurement, what is the 'Point of Total Assumption' (PTA) in a Fixed Price Incentive Fee (FPIF) contract?", "The cost point beyond which the contractor assumes 100% of all additional cost overruns dollar-for-dollar", ["The moment when the buyer pays the final invoice", "The point where the contractor achieves maximum incentive bonus", "The initial cost estimate agreed at contract signature"], "PTA = [(Ceiling Price - Target Price) / Buyer Share Ratio] + Target Cost. Beyond PTA, the contractor bears all overrun burden."),
    ("In an FPIF contract with Target Cost = $100k, Target Fee = $10k, Ceiling Price = $120k, Buyer Share = 80%, what is the PTA?", "$112,500", ["$120,000", "$110,000", "$105,000"], "PTA = Target Cost + (Ceiling Price - Target Price) / Buyer Share = 100,000 + (120,000 - 110,000) / 0.80 = 100,000 + 12,500 = $112,500."),
    ("Under the Law of Diminishing Returns in project resource management:", "Adding more resources beyond optimal capacity yields progressively smaller increases in output", ["Adding staff to a late project makes it finish early", "Project costs decrease exponentially as team size doubles", "Overtime work produces identical productivity as normal hours"], "Excessive staffing increases communication overhead, coordination complexity, and interference, reducing marginal productivity."),
    ("What does 'Brooks' Law' famously state regarding software project staffing?", "'Adding manpower to a late software project makes it later'", ["'Doubling developers halves project schedule duration'", "'No software project can ever finish under original budget'", "'Quality increases proportionally to the number of testers'"], "Coined by Fred Brooks in 'The Mythical Man-Month', ramp-up communication overhead and training drain existing productivity."),
    ("In the Vroom-Yetton-Jago Decision Model, a project leader selects a decision-making style based on:", "A decision tree evaluating decision quality, commitment requirements, problem structure, and team goal congruence", ["The Myers-Briggs personality profiles of team members", "The current CPI and SPI metrics from Earned Value reports", "The personal seniority rank of the project manager"], "The model prescribes whether a leader should decide autocratically, consultatively, or facilitate consensus based on diagnostic questions."),
    ("What is 'Cynefin Framework' used for in executive project leadership and decision-making?", "Categorizing problems into Clear, Complicated, Complex, and Chaotic contexts to adopt appropriate responses", ["Calculating statistical variance in three-point cost estimates", "Measuring database throughput in transaction processing systems", "Auditing vendor invoices against contractual milestone triggers"], "Developed by Dave Snowden, Cynefin guides whether to Sense-Categorize-Respond, Sense-Analyze, Sense-Probe, or Act immediately."),
    ("In Agile scaling frameworks such as SAFe (Scaled Agile Framework), what is a 'Program Increment' (PI)?", "A timeboxed planning cycle (typically 8 to 12 weeks) where an entire Agile Release Train plans and delivers value", ["The promotion of an individual developer to scrum master", "The release of a minor security patch to staging servers", "A weekly sprint conducted by senior management"], "A PI aligns multiple Agile teams working together on an Agile Release Train (ART) via common cadence and PI Planning."),
    ("What is the primary role of the 'Release Train Engineer' (RTE) in the Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe)?", "Serving as the chief scrum master who facilitates ART events and drives program-level continuous improvement", ["Writing code for backend microservices pipelines", "Deciding the annual marketing spend for enterprise products", "Signing commercial vendor contracts with hardware suppliers"], "The RTE acts as a servant leader facilitating PI execution, escalating impediments, and managing ART risks."),
    ("In Scrum, how is the 'Sprint Goal' formally utilized by the Scrum Team?", "As a single overarching objective set during Sprint Planning that guides team decisions throughout the sprint", ["As a fixed legal quota that results in salary deductions if missed", "As an optional guideline created by external marketing executives", "As a marketing slogan printed on office promotional posters"], "The Sprint Goal provides coherence and focus, encouraging teamwork rather than disjointed individual user story work."),
    ("What is the 'Kano Model' in project product management?", "A customer satisfaction model categorizing features into Must-be, Performance, Attractive, and Indifferent", ["A statistical tool for predicting server downtime under peak load", "A contract drafting framework for international marine shipping", "A project scheduling algorithm that calculates free float"], "Noriaki Kano mapped how feature presence versus customer satisfaction distinguishes baseline expectations from delighters."),
    ("In organizational design, how does a 'Strong Matrix' organization differ from a 'Weak Matrix'?", "Project Managers have primary authority over budget and resources in Strong Matrix; Functional Managers dominate in Weak Matrix", ["Strong Matrix has zero full-time project managers", "Weak Matrix assigns all project decisions to external clients", "Strong Matrix is only permitted in public sector government bodies"], "In a strong matrix, dedicated PMs hold substantial authority, whereas in weak matrix, functional heads manage projects via coordinators."),
    ("What is a 'Project Management Office' (PMO) designated as 'Directive' characterized by?", "It directly manages projects, assigns project managers, and holds high degree of project control", ["It provides optional templates and acts purely as a consultative resource", "It only audits tax returns at the end of the corporate fiscal year", "It is an external consulting firm hired for one single audit"], "PMO models range from Supportive (low control) and Controlling (moderate compliance) to Directive (direct command and management)."),
    ("In Agile frameworks, what is the 'Weighted Shortest Job First' (WSJF) prioritization metric based on?", "Cost of Delay (CoD) divided by Job Duration or Size", ["Total Estimated Revenue divided by Developer Salaries", "Risk Probability multiplied by Financial Monetary Impact", "Customer Seniority Level divided by Sprint Length"], "WSJF optimizes economic value flow by prioritizing high-value, high-urgency, short-duration backlog initiatives first."),
    ("In Earned Value Management, if CV is positive and SV is negative, what does this indicate about project status?", "The project is currently under budget but behind schedule", ["The project is over budget and ahead of schedule", "The project is over budget and behind schedule", "The project is performing exactly as planned"], "CV = EV - AC > 0 means costs are lower than value earned (under budget); SV = EV - PV < 0 means behind schedule."),
    ("What is 'Fast Tracking' rework risk primarily attributed to?", "Prematurely starting subsequent activities before their predecessor designs or prerequisites are validated", ["Assigning too many junior engineers to high-complexity coding tasks", "Exceeding the statutory working hours permitted by labor laws", "Paying vendors earlier than required by contractual milestones"], "Fast-tracking introduces rework because unforeseen changes in the predecessor task may invalidate progress on overlapping successor work."),
    ("In project risk management, what is a 'Risk Breakdown Structure' (RBS)?", "A hierarchical depiction of potential project risks organized by risk category and sub-category", ["A financial spreadsheet detailing money lost to workplace accidents", "A breakdown of contractor penalty fees for delayed deliveries", "A chronological timeline of project bugs logged in Jira"], "An RBS organizes risks hierarchically (Technical, External, Organizational, Project Management) to aid comprehensive identification."),
    ("What is the primary function of a 'Tornado Diagram' in quantitative project risk analysis?", "A sensitivity analysis display that ranks variables by their relative impact on a project outcome", ["A weather forecast chart tracking tropical hurricane paths", "A scatter plot showing developer productivity versus room temperature", "A cumulative distribution curve of total project capital expend"], "Tornado diagrams visually rank high-impact sensitivity variables at the top and low-impact at the bottom, resembling a tornado funnel."),
    ("What is 'Benefit-Cost Ratio' (BCR) and when is an investment financially attractive?", "BCR = Present Value of Benefits / Present Value of Costs; project is attractive when BCR > 1.0", ["BCR = Total Incurred Costs / Net Profit; project is attractive when BCR < 0.5", "BCR = Revenue - Cost; project is attractive when BCR = 0", "BCR = Depreciation Rate / Inflation Rate; attractive when BCR > 2.0"], "A BCR greater than 1.0 indicates that the financial present value of benefits exceeds the present value of investment costs."),
    ("In project financial appraisal, what does 'Internal Rate of Return' (IRR) represent?", "The discount rate at which the Net Present Value (NPV) of all project cash flows equals zero", ["The annual rate of interest charged by commercial lending banks", "The statutory corporate tax percentage payable on project profits", "The inflation adjustment applied to employee annual bonuses"], "A project is viable if its IRR exceeds the organization's required hurdle rate or cost of capital."),
    ("In project scheduling, what is the 'Dangler' flaw in network logic?", "An activity that has no predecessor or no successor, leaving an open-ended loop in the network", ["A resource assigned to three full-time projects simultaneously", "A milestone that has a non-zero financial expenditure", "A critical path that passes through a third-party vendor task"], "Danglers break schedule calculations by preventing forward and backward passes from calculating true floats."),
    ("What is 'Murphy's Law' as applied to project risk planning?", "'Anything that can go wrong will go wrong'", ["'Work expands to fill the time available for its completion'", "'Nine women cannot make a baby in one month'", "'Adding staff to a late project makes it later'"], "Murphy's Law reminds project managers to design resilient systems, incorporate contingency buffers, and plan defensive mitigations.")
]
